WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:06.480
It had been getting slower; the longer the&nbsp;
days, weeks, months, and years went on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:14.580
the more time it took to move. Before, long before&nbsp;
the collapse, it used to move like lightning. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:20.220
the blink of a human eye, it could go from one&nbsp;
side of a room to the other. It had moved with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:25.860
such speed that it looked to have disappeared&nbsp;
from one place and reappeared in another.&nbsp;

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:32.940
By the time the same human’s eye reopened,&nbsp;
there it would be, standing before them. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:38.700
as they realized that they had to strain, keep&nbsp;
their eyes open and stare at it, it would wait&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:46.740
until they inevitably had to blink again. That was&nbsp;
when it struck. But now its movements were getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:52.080
sluggish. It had been able to move around of its&nbsp;
own volition for some time after the collapse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:59.100
unobserved and thus free for the first time in&nbsp;
a while. Yet, now it was succumbing to the one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:05.820
thing it couldn’t move faster than; the one thing&nbsp;
that makes dust of all things eventually: time.&nbsp;

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:14.160
A sound, suddenly and faintly, but undoubtedly&nbsp;
caused by movement. A human… a victim. It turned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:20.880
to see the man enter the room, stopping still as&nbsp;
his gaze fell over it. All it had to do was wait…&nbsp;

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:25.560
The agent could barely believe his eyes.&nbsp;
The second he’d spotted The Sculpture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:32.100
he’d instinctively frozen to the spot, rigid with&nbsp;
sheer terror, heart drumming against his ribcage.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:38.040
Standing across the room from it, directly&nbsp;
opposite, he kept himself as still as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:43.140
concrete and rebar statue was; the agent knew&nbsp;
that, as long as he looked at it, it couldn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:49.080
move. He just had to make sure he didn’t so much&nbsp;
as blink… or at least, that’s how it used to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:55.980
work. To his horror, the agent noticed the arm&nbsp;
of SCP-173 start to slowly reach towards him…&nbsp;

00:01:55.980 --> 00:02:02.520
But how? How had The Sculpture, an anomaly&nbsp;
only capable of moving while unobserved,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:08.640
found a way to suddenly start reaching for this&nbsp;
agent? Well, for a start, that has something to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:16.260
do with the particular Foundation operative who&nbsp;
was looking at SCP-173… and technically wasn’t at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:22.680
the same time. Let’s start at the beginning.
You see, some time ago, a former field agent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:29.340
working at the SCP Foundation suffered quite an&nbsp;
unprecedented accident. Let’s call him Daryl,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:35.160
a seemingly average man in his thirties. Well, as&nbsp;
average as someone working for the Foundation can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:43.620
be. However, one mission would unexpectedly change&nbsp;
Daryl’s life forever – turning him into SCP-451.&nbsp;

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:48.960
It started as a straightforward retrieval&nbsp;
operation. Daryl was instructed to recover&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:54.420
a dangerous anomalous artifact, however, he&nbsp;
ultimately failed and was declared missing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:59.280
in action as a result. That is, until he&nbsp;
miraculously resurfaced a month later at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:04.380
Site 19. But from Daryl’s point of view, the&nbsp;
entire Foundation facility looked like it had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:09.240
been deserted. Corridors and labs that had&nbsp;
previously been bustling hives of activity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:15.180
were now unsettlingly empty. SCPs seemed&nbsp;
to still be in their containment chambers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:22.320
but without anyone watching or guarding them.
As far as SCP-451 knew, he was the only man still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:28.440
alive on Earth. Some anomalous influence&nbsp;
had left him with a unique affliction: A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:35.700
permanently altered perception. Daryl was rendered&nbsp;
incapable of seeing other human beings. It wasn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:41.760
just limited to his sight either, he couldn’t&nbsp;
hear anyone or even perceive their existence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:48.000
Any attempts the Foundation’s researchers made&nbsp;
to communicate with SCP-451 were unsuccessful;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:54.360
they could still see him, but it was like he was&nbsp;
absent, standing just outside the real world.&nbsp;

00:03:54.360 --> 00:04:00.000
And yet, to Daryl, it was somehow worse than&nbsp;
that. Given the lack of people around him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:06.480
being unable to see, hear or interact with&nbsp;
anyone, SCP-451 quickly adopted the belief&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:12.060
that by interacting with the anomalous artifact,&nbsp;
he had single-handedly wiped out the entire human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:17.460
race. While this wasn’t true, he had no way&nbsp;
of knowing that everyone else was still alive;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.460 --> 00:04:23.100
he just couldn’t perceive them. The guilt&nbsp;
instilled in Daryl weighed heavily on the agent’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:29.520
conscience. He spent a lot of time wandering the&nbsp;
corridors of Site 19, overwhelmed by his apparent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:34.980
isolation, convinced he’d single-handedly&nbsp;
caused the extinction of the human race.&nbsp;

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:42.420
But what does SCP-451 have to do with SCP-173?&nbsp;
How did Daryl go from being unable to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.420 --> 00:04:47.520
and hear other humans to staring down The&nbsp;
Sculpture? Well, while Daryl was suffering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:52.740
from his newly altered perception, something&nbsp;
catastrophic was unfolding, in the wider&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:58.800
world he could hardly interact with anymore.
An End of the World Scenario had taken place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:03.600
and left barely any survivors, making&nbsp;
the perceived extinction of humanity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:10.260
by SCP-451 oddly prophetic. Something had&nbsp;
caused the human race to actually die out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:16.140
and with nobody around who could explain what had&nbsp;
happened, the actual how and why were forgotten&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:23.400
to history. And even if someone had managed to&nbsp;
survive, SCP-451 would still be as oblivious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:29.640
to their explanations as he was to the disaster&nbsp;
that claimed the lives of not only the whole SCP&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:35.760
Foundation, but the rest of the world too.
Of course, without the Foundation and its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:40.620
personnel around any longer to safeguard the&nbsp;
rest of humanity – or even anything left of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:46.740
humanity for them to protect – things quickly&nbsp;
got even worse. The various contained SCPs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.740 --> 00:05:52.740
soon found themselves free to run amok. Whatever&nbsp;
had wiped the human race from existence left the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.740 --> 00:05:58.860
world mostly uninhabited, and it didn’t take long&nbsp;
for some of the more aggressive anomalies to turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.860 --> 00:06:04.740
on each other. With no humans for them to prey&nbsp;
on, they had to make do. But while they fought,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.740 --> 00:06:09.300
killed, and died in the ruins of&nbsp;
the world, one was left to roam&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:15.900
freer than it had ever been: SCP-173.
Now that the Sculpture was no longer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.900 --> 00:06:20.520
caged by the Foundation, it could&nbsp;
move around unobserved. Before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:25.500
it would be frozen in place whenever personnel&nbsp;
entered its cell to clean it every two weeks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:31.920
constantly watching it to stop it from moving. But&nbsp;
now, there were no personnel. After the human race&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:38.580
had been wiped out, SCP-173 set about bursting&nbsp;
free from its container. It took quite some time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:44.700
using its heavy concrete and rebar arms to dent&nbsp;
the walls from within, continually pounding on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.700 --> 00:06:50.700
the metal until it eventually gave way.
Bursting free, SCP-173 found itself in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.700 --> 00:06:55.620
world with hardly anyone around to look at it –&nbsp;
and that meant it could go anywhere it wanted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.160 --> 00:07:02.100
do anything it pleased, so long as none of&nbsp;
the other surviving SCPs came across it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.100 --> 00:07:07.380
That happened from time to time, of course.&nbsp;
Any of the more humanoid anomalies that were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.380 --> 00:07:12.960
capable of sight would sometimes stumble upon&nbsp;
the Sculpture. But they had to blink sometime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:20.460
and when they did, SCP-173 would effortlessly zip&nbsp;
over to where they stood and break their necks.&nbsp;

00:07:20.460 --> 00:07:27.900
Exercising its newfound freedom, SCP-173 moved&nbsp;
unnoticed to the office of Doctor Alto Clef,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.900 --> 00:07:33.420
the infamously unhinged Foundation&nbsp;
researcher, weapons fanatic, and specialist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.420 --> 00:07:40.860
in ‘decommissioning’ SCPs. Clef, like the rest of&nbsp;
the human race, was nowhere to be found when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:46.860
Sculpture burst into his office. If it had the&nbsp;
capacity for emotions, maybe it felt angered,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:52.860
even cheated to find that Clef was gone. After&nbsp;
all, it seemed to have come seeking revenge.&nbsp;

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:58.800
For a number of years, Doctor Clef had been&nbsp;
infatuated with SCP-173. Were he still alive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:05.040
Clef himself would have described the anomalous&nbsp;
statue as a former flame, someone he had a bond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:11.760
with. Although he had no idea how one-sided his&nbsp;
feelings truly were. The Sculpture, if it could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:17.400
feel, would have been indifferent towards the&nbsp;
crazed researcher… were it not for ‘Date Night’.&nbsp;

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:24.480
Clef had, on a number of occasions, dressed&nbsp;
SCP-173 in a black dress and blonde wig,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:30.780
and shared romantic evenings in its company.&nbsp;
It’s never been known if SCP-173 is just an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.780 --> 00:08:36.360
object with anomalous movement properties, or if&nbsp;
the Sculpture is an actual living creature with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.360 --> 00:08:42.660
sentience. But if it was, then no doubt being made&nbsp;
to go on repeated dates with Doctor Clef would’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.660 --> 00:08:49.560
humiliated SCP-173. And as if searched Clef’s&nbsp;
office, while it couldn’t find the loathsome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:55.680
researcher, it did find a certain black dress&nbsp;
and wig… that the Sculpture quickly crushed under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.680 --> 00:09:01.380
the weight of its heavy, stony arms. It wasn’t&nbsp;
Doctor Clef being smashed, but it was enough.&nbsp;

00:09:01.380 --> 00:09:08.280
Meanwhile, SCP-451 still remained completely&nbsp;
unaware that everyone else was gone. To him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:13.860
nothing had changed; he still couldn’t perceive&nbsp;
anyone, not that there was anyone around anyway.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.860 --> 00:09:18.960
He’d witnessed a lot of the destruction unfurled&nbsp;
by the anomalies breaking their containment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:25.080
but most didn’t pay Daryl any mind. Ones that&nbsp;
did quickly found they couldn’t interact with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:30.120
him or cause the former Foundation agent&nbsp;
harm, and quickly lost interest in him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.120 --> 00:09:36.000
That was an added, torturous part of&nbsp;
SCP-451’s condition: he couldn’t die.&nbsp;

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:41.820
A long time before the End of the World Scenario,&nbsp;
Daryl had found a firearm belonging to another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.820 --> 00:09:48.480
member of Foundation personnel in the main&nbsp;
breakroom of Site 19. As SCP-451 tried to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:53.580
fire the weapon, the bullet richoted off the&nbsp;
wall, and the gunshot passed directly through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.580 --> 00:09:59.280
him. The same bullet mortally wounded another&nbsp;
nearby member of Foundation staff. And it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.280 --> 00:10:06.120
after he’d accidentally injured this unsuspecting&nbsp;
researcher that SCP-451 found he was briefly able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.120 --> 00:10:12.240
to interact with them, right as the researcher’s&nbsp;
life was fading. That was the last time he’d been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.240 --> 00:10:20.820
able to see anyone. Well, any human anyway.
But then, he encountered SCP-173. The pair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.820 --> 00:10:26.160
of them came face to face with each other in&nbsp;
the ruins of a Foundation site. By that point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.160 --> 00:10:31.680
the overwhelming loneliness of believing himself&nbsp;
to be humanity’s sole survivor had already taken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.680 --> 00:10:37.500
a bad toll on Daryl. He was losing himself,&nbsp;
every day getting more and more overwhelmed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.500 --> 00:10:42.660
by the anomalous hell he was living.
SCP-173 was caught for a moment when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.660 --> 00:10:48.960
SCP-451 walked in on it, stuck fast like it&nbsp;
had been whenever someone observed it. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.960 --> 00:10:54.780
as Daryl stared and tried not to blink, the&nbsp;
Sculpture found itself still able to move. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.780 --> 00:11:01.260
could see SCP-451, and he could see it… yet&nbsp;
it could still move. Somehow, due to the way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.260 --> 00:11:07.260
Daryl’s perception had been anomalously altered,&nbsp;
it affected the way he saw The Sculpture. It was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.260 --> 00:11:13.440
almost like he was seeing it, without seeing.
It had been so long since humanity disappeared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:18.660
that the Sculpture’s movements had been gradually&nbsp;
getting slower. The rebar holding it together had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.660 --> 00:11:23.820
been rusting, creaking more as it bent and&nbsp;
twisted. Its concrete parts were starting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.820 --> 00:11:30.240
to crumble away. And now, despite being free,&nbsp;
it was struggling just to reach an arm out to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.240 --> 00:11:37.140
SCP-451 to kill him. For his part, SCP-451 had&nbsp;
been forced to live out a painful existence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.140 --> 00:11:45.060
spending every day alone. As delirium had begun&nbsp;
to sink its claws into him, Daryl had wished for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.060 --> 00:11:52.680
one thing: some company… and now he’d found it.
Misinterpreting SCP-173’s arm extending towards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.680 --> 00:11:59.820
him, SCP-451 broke down crying and embraced&nbsp;
the Sculpture. His tears ran down the anomalous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.820 --> 00:12:05.280
statue’s decaying concrete body, the struts in&nbsp;
its arms creaking as it tried to reach for Daryl.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:11.340
But the Sculpture was getting old, too old to&nbsp;
kill as quick as it used to. Time and lowliness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.340 --> 00:12:17.820
two of the most painful things to experience&nbsp;
the side effects of. And yet, while SCP-173&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.820 --> 00:12:23.640
continued to wither, it was unknowingly&nbsp;
helping alleviate someone else’s pain.&nbsp;

00:12:23.640 --> 00:12:30.900
Although it could still move while SCP-451 looked&nbsp;
at it, Daryl didn’t leave the Sculpture alone. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.900 --> 00:12:38.760
his mind, driven half-mad by being isolated for&nbsp;
so long, he genuinely believed that SCP-173 and he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.760 --> 00:12:46.200
were friends. The Sculpture had spared him; after&nbsp;
all, SCP-451 had no idea what had happened to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.200 --> 00:12:51.480
rest of the human race, and the other SCPs now&nbsp;
loose on the world barely acknowledged him. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.480 --> 00:13:00.660
in the concrete and rebar statue, he had a friend.
SCP-451 followed SCP-173 as it shuffled itself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.660 --> 00:13:07.260
away, not leaving its side, matching the slowed&nbsp;
speed of its stony, scraping movements. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.260 --> 00:13:11.040
they wandered together, Daryl poured his&nbsp;
heart out about everything and anything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.040 --> 00:13:17.100
glad to have someone- anyone – to talk&nbsp;
to, even if the Sculpture couldn’t reply.&nbsp;

00:13:17.100 --> 00:13:22.800
Again, nobody knows for sure if SCP-173&nbsp;
was an object or a living creature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.800 --> 00:13:29.700
but if it was the latter, then there’s still no&nbsp;
telling how it perceived SCP-451. It could have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.700 --> 00:13:35.340
taken pity on him, maybe sympathizing with&nbsp;
his plight and begrudgingly alleviating his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.340 --> 00:13:40.920
solitude. Or it might have found Daryl to be a&nbsp;
nuisance. We’ll never know, and neither would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.920 --> 00:13:49.140
SCP-451. As the years passed, SCP-173 continued&nbsp;
to slow down, and kept withering. Portions of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.140 --> 00:13:54.900
its concrete body crumbled away until they were&nbsp;
little more than dust. The metal wired through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.900 --> 00:14:01.860
Sculpture rusted, eventually coming to a stop.
What was left of SCP-173 stopped moving,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.860 --> 00:14:08.280
fixed to one spot where it would stay.&nbsp;
Forever still. Not realizing it had perished,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.280 --> 00:14:16.920
SCP-451 sat beside it and kept talking.
Now go check out “SCP-096 VS. SCP-173”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.920 --> 00:14:23.520
and “SCP-173 ORIGIN STORY - HOW 173 GOT&nbsp;
TO SITE-19”? for more killer Sculpture!

